2.25.1

 local/remote boundary

sysObjectID came back as ISS's generic .1.3.6.1.2.1 instead of the Moxa
product OID .1.3.6.1.4.1.8691.600.1.5.4: the entry carries
GENERATE_SYS_OID and is computed by snmpd itself, so ISS cannot serve
it. Same class as the synthesised-index regression fixed in 2ed5f03,
and it appeared at the same place in the same way - which is the point.
Enumerating flags at the top of mox_snmp_handle_entry() had already
failed once.

The forwarding therefore no longer sits at the top of the GET path. It
now sits inside entry_handle_generate_iss_value_file() and
entry_handle_generate_uri_value_file(), in the else-branch of the local
value producer, immediately before the call that would leave the
process. Everything reachable before that point - the in-process
callbacks behind ChkEntryGetValue(), URI_FIXED_VALUE, GENERATE_SYS_OID
- is local by construction, so a local producer added later is not
forwarded without anyone having to remember a list.

Both generators take reginfo/reqinfo/request and return the new
IES_GET_FORWARDED, on which the caller answers NOERROR and stops.

SYNTHETIC_INDEX stays an explicit guard on the forward. It is local for
a different reason: the value does come from ISS, but the column only
exists after iss_snmp_makeup_index() has run locally, so no subagent can
serve it.

Two side effects of moving the forward past the cache check:
  - unlink(fname) + util_table_snapshot_reset() now run before each
    forwarded GET. The unlink drops stale local value files, which is
    the safer direction; the reset can cost a redundant ISS read in a
    walk that mixes forwarded and local tables.
  - a table whose snapshot is still inside the 800 ms window is served
    from that snapshot instead of being forwarded. Only reachable after
    a local fallback has already happened, and it self-heals.

ret in both generators was uninitialised on the paths that return
mox_snmp_flask_handle_result(..., ret); harmless before, but it could
now surface as a bogus IES_GET_FORWARDED. Initialised to IES_ERR.

Host-gcc syntax check with the target's flags is clean. Cross-compile
and DUT verification are the operator's step.

